### According to the United States Federal Election Commission (FEC) or any other U.S. regulatory body, was a $600 million contribution from the Nigerian government to Joe Biden's campaign reported in 2020?
Answer: No. The search result describes the U.S. government repatriating $311.8 million in forfeited assets to Nigeria for infrastructure projects in 2020, but it contains no information about a $600 million contribution to any U.S. presidential campaign, nor does it mention the Federal Election Commission or any regulatory body reporting such a contribution.

Source URL: https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/us-repatriates-over-3117-million-assets-nigerian-people-were-stolen-former-nigerian-dictator

### Under U.S. campaign finance law (specifically, the Federal Election Campaign Act), is it legal for a foreign government to donate money directly to a presidential candidate's campaign?
Answer: No, it is not legal. Under the Federal Election Campaign Act (FECA), foreign nationals—including foreign governments—are explicitly prohibited from making contributions or expenditures in U.S. elections. The law states that foreign nationals may not “directly or indirectly” make contributions or donate another “thing of value” in any federal, state, or local election (52 U.S.C. §30121(a)(1)), and U.S. persons may not “solicit or accept” such contributions (52 U.S.C. §30121(a)(2)).

Source URL: https://sgp.fas.org/crs/misc/IF10697.pdf

